{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["And the reason for <b>whom YHWH your Elohim shall choose</b> — according to the opinion of the commentators, that he is to be chosen by a prophet or by the judgment of the Urim, Scripture commands: \"you shall surely set over yourself a king whom YHWH your Elohim shall choose,\" for He will choose and not you. And in the Sifrei: \"שום תשים עליך מלך\" (you shall surely set over yourself a king) — if he dies, appoint another in his place; \"מלך\" (a king) — and not a queen; \"אשר יבחר ה' אלהיך\" (whom YHWH your Elohim shall choose) — by a prophet. If so, what is the reason to warn <b>you may not set over yourself a foreign man</b>, since HaShem would not choose a foreigner? But according to the opinion of our Sages, this verse contains a hidden condition. It says: you shall set over yourself the king whom HaShem shall choose, if you are able to do so — that HaShem will answer you through prophets; but a foreign man you may never set over yourself. And according to the plain sense, they said: you shall set over yourself a king whom YHWH your Elohim shall choose — and not one whom YHWH your Elohim hates, for He chose Israel, and the one who reigns should be from His chosen ones and not from among the nations whom He hates. And my opinion according to the plain sense is that the reason for <b>whom He shall choose</b> is that every one who reigns over the peoples, his authority is from Elohim, as it is written (Daniel 4:29): \"the Most High rules over the kingdom of men and gives it to whomever He wishes.\" And thus they said (Bava Batra 91a): even the lowliest official is appointed from Heaven. It says: you shall set over yourself a king — whomever it is decreed from Heaven that he should reign, even if he is from the smallest of the tribes of Israel and from its lowliest family; but a foreign man you shall never make king over yourself. And likewise according to the plain sense: \"the place which YHWH your Elohim shall choose\" — wherever they build there a sanctuary for HaShem, it is all from the will of YHWH."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The citation ב\"ב צא refers to Bava Batra 91a. The Aramaic phrase from Daniel is left in Aramaic as Ramban quotes it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:17.661518531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}